Experiment 2: Develop a program to interface a relay with Arduino
Board

Algorithm: Interfacing a Relay with Arduino UNO
1.	Start the program. 
2.	Declare the relay control pin (Digital Pin 7). 
3.	Initialize the relay pin as an OUTPUT using the pinMode() function. 
4.	Set the relay to the OFF state by writing LOW to the relay pin. 
5.	Turn ON the relay by writing HIGH to the relay pin. 
6.	Wait for 2 seconds using the delay(2000) function. 
7.	Turn OFF the relay by writing LOW to the relay pin. 
8.	Wait for 2 seconds using the delay(2000) function. 
9.	Repeat Steps 5–8 continuously to switch the relay ON and OFF. 
10.	End (The program runs continuously inside the loop() function, so it does not terminate.)
Working Principle
1.	Arduino supplies a control signal to the relay module through Digital Pin 7. 
2.	When the output is HIGH, the relay coil is energized. 
3.	The relay switch closes the Normally Open (NO) contact. 
4.	The connected load receives power and turns ON. 
5.	After 2 seconds, Arduino outputs LOW. 
6.	The relay coil is de-energized. 
7.	The switch opens, disconnecting the load. 
8.	The ON–OFF cycle repeats continuously.
Arduino Program with Line-by-Line Explanation
// Relay Interfacing with Arduino UNO

const int relayPin = 7;          // Declare Digital Pin 7 as the relay control pin.

void setup()
{
    pinMode(relayPin, OUTPUT);   // Configure relay pin as an OUTPUT pin.

    digitalWrite(relayPin, LOW); // Initially keep the relay OFF.
                                 // LOW de-energizes the relay coil.
}

void loop()
{
    digitalWrite(relayPin, HIGH); // Send HIGH (5V) to the relay module.
                                  // Relay is energized and the connected load turns ON.

    delay(2000);                  // Keep the relay ON for 2 seconds.

    digitalWrite(relayPin, LOW);  // Send LOW (0V) to the relay module.
                                  // Relay is de-energized and the connected load turns OFF.

    delay(2000);                  // Keep the relay OFF for 2 seconds before repeating.

}                                 // End of loop(). The Arduino automatically executes
                                  // loop() repeatedly, so the relay keeps switching
                                  // ON and OFF continuously.

Expected Output
•	The relay clicks ON for 2 seconds. 
•	The relay clicks OFF for 2 seconds. 
•	If a load (LED or bulb) is connected through the relay, it will blink ON and OFF every 2 seconds. 
