Viva Questions with Answers – Relay Interfacing with Arduino UNO
1. What is a relay?
Answer:
A relay is an electrically operated switch that allows a low-voltage circuit (Arduino) to control a high-voltage or high-current device while providing electrical isolation.
________________________________________
2. Why is a relay used with Arduino?
Answer:
A relay is used to control AC or high-power DC appliances such as bulbs, fans, motors, and pumps using the low-voltage digital output of the Arduino.
________________________________________
3. What are the main terminals of a relay module?
Answer:
•	VCC – Power supply (5V) 
•	GND – Ground 
•	IN – Control input from Arduino 
•	COM – Common terminal 
•	NO – Normally Open contact 
•	NC – Normally Closed contact 
________________________________________
4. What is the difference between NO and NC contacts?
Answer:
NO (Normally Open)	NC (Normally Closed)
Open when relay is OFF	Closed when relay is OFF
Closes when relay is ON	Opens when relay is ON
________________________________________
5. Which Arduino pin is used in this experiment?
Answer:
Digital Pin 7 is used to control the relay.
________________________________________
6. What is the purpose of pinMode()?
Answer:
pinMode() configures an Arduino pin as either INPUT or OUTPUT.
________________________________________
7. What happens when digitalWrite(relayPin, HIGH) is executed?
Answer:
The relay coil is energized, the relay switches its contacts, and the connected load turns ON.
________________________________________
8. What happens when digitalWrite(relayPin, LOW) is executed?
Answer:
The relay coil is de-energized, the contacts return to their normal position, and the connected load turns OFF.
________________________________________
9. What is the function of delay(2000)?
Answer:
It pauses the program execution for 2000 milliseconds (2 seconds).
________________________________________
10. Why is electrical isolation important in relay modules?
Answer:
Electrical isolation protects the Arduino and the user from high-voltage circuits by separating the control circuit from the load circuit.
________________________________________
11. Can Arduino directly control a 230V AC bulb?
Answer:
No. Arduino operates at 5V and cannot directly control 230V AC. A relay module is required to safely switch the AC load.
________________________________________
12. What is the operating voltage of an Arduino UNO?
Answer:
The Arduino UNO operates at 5V.
________________________________________
13. Why is a relay module preferred over a bare relay?
Answer:
A relay module includes a transistor driver, flyback diode, and often an optocoupler, making it easier and safer to interface with Arduino.
________________________________________
14. What is the purpose of the flyback diode in a relay module?
Answer:
The flyback diode protects the Arduino and driver circuit from the high reverse voltage (back EMF) generated when the relay coil is switched OFF.
________________________________________
